WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:02.070
MARTIN: Hi, Kevin.

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:03.360
KEVIN: Hi, Martin.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.370
MARTIN: I heard you
work for a car sharing

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.039
service called GreenMobility,
and the company

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:09.660
is using serverless.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.970
KEVIN: Yeah, our dev
team is really productive

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.040
thanks to serverless computing.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.840
MARTIN: Oh, maybe you
could share some tips

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.240
on how to use serverless
to get your developers more

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.230
productive?

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.590
KEVIN: I would be happy to.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:24.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:34.960
MARTIN: So, Kevin, GreenMobility
is a car sharing service?

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:35.770
KEVIN: Yes.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:39.070
We run a car sharing platform
in seven European cities.

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:40.870
We have hundreds of cars.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.870
And people use them for
[INAUDIBLE] trips every day.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.870
We have about 100
employees, with headquarters

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:51.000
in Copenhagen. And we are listed
on the NASDAQ main market.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.330
MARTIN: Oh, cool.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:55.860
I guess many people want to
use cars, but not necessarily

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.550
own cars these days.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:58.050
KEVIN: Yeah.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.180
Like, more and more people
want to live in cities.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.250
And they're interested
in sustainability.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:06.520
And they also prefer sharing
things rather than owning them.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:07.930
MARTIN: Oh, right.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.450
So what kind of data
processing does the company

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:12.517
need to make this
car sharing happen?

00:01:12.517 --> 00:01:14.350
KEVIN: We've processed
hundreds of thousands

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:16.000
of events every day.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.195
Trips, stars, car
moves, a car trip ends.

00:01:20.195 --> 00:01:21.820
We could have built
this infrastructure

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.603
for handling these events
with virtual machines.

00:01:24.603 --> 00:01:26.270
But there would have
been a lot of work.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:27.645
Instead, we went
with serverless.

00:01:27.645 --> 00:01:29.770
It make this much easier.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:30.760
MARTIN: Oh, cool.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.590
I love serverless.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.540
Where do you store
these events, Kevin?

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:36.550
KEVIN: We store
them in Firestore.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:39.010
We also have a Cloud SQL
database for other data.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.410
But we got better data
writing performance

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.300
at a lower cost with Firestore.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.910
And Firestore is serverless,
which means less iteration

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:46.780
work for us.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.660
So we will have SQL
and NoSQL databases.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.100
MARTIN: Ah, got it.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.180
Machine learning
is hot right now.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:54.910
Are you doing that, too?

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:56.440
KEVIN: We're working on it.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.460
We like to predict where
cars will be needed,

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.630
so we can put cars in
those places ahead of time.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.300
Our plan is to export the
Firestore database to BigQuery

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.560
every night.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.240
Then we can run
reports in BigQuery

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:12.170
and run BigQuery ML to do the
machine learning on the data.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:13.030
MARTIN: Oh.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.850
Are there any other factors that
made you go with serverless?

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:17.950
KEVIN: We also
have uneven demand.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.920
More people are driving
during the day than at night.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.200
So it would be inefficient
to scale our computing

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.720
and databases up
and down ourselves.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:28.510
But Cloud Run and Firestore
do it automatically.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:29.470
MARTIN: Ah.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.740
Why did you pick Cloud
Run specifically, Kevin?

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.270
KEVIN: We really
like containers.

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:35.890
And Cloud Run lets
us host containers

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:38.410
without having to
worry about the server.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.760
If a server is acting up,
Google fixes the problem

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.040
and we don't have to.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.570
Also, we like the billing model.

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:45.715
We pay for container time,
even if the container

00:02:45.715 --> 00:02:49.120
is handling multiple
requests at the same time.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.530
MARTIN: You mentioned billing.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:53.240
How much are you actually
paying for Cloud Run?

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.220
KEVIN: Our Cloud Run
bill has been growing.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.470
So I'm keeping an eye on it.

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:58.870
Last month, we handled
six million requests.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:01.630
With Cloud Run, we paid
2,000 Danish kroner,

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:05.140
and that's about
$320 US dollars.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.510
MARTIN: Not too bad.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.202
How do you deploy your
containers to Cloud Run?

00:03:09.202 --> 00:03:11.410
KEVIN: When a [INAUDIBLE]
merges into the main branch

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:13.990
in our source control, a
trigger ultimately builds

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.840
and deploys the container
to our staging environment.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.090
And that happens
about 50 times a week.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.490
And when we're done testing
that particular release,

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:23.800
we manually run a script
to deploy that container

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.130
to our production environment.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:27.275
And that also happens
about 50 times per week.

00:03:27.275 --> 00:03:28.900
MARTIN: Are you
building the containers

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:31.660
on one of your machines,
or up in the cloud?

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:34.432
KEVIN: How are we using cloud
built-in Google Cloud platform?

00:03:34.432 --> 00:03:35.140
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:39.100
Our build was recently getting
slow and taking 15 minutes.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.380
But it was easy for us to
change that machine type

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.780
in one line on our
cloud builder YAML,

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:47.150
which reduced the build
time to five minutes only.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:48.590
MARTIN: Oh, nice.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.040
So, when you do 50 deployments
per week, that's a lot.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.620
How do you keep these
deployments straight.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:57.170
KEVIN: We're big fans of
immutable deployments.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:58.880
When Cloud Run
deploys a container,

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.370
you can't tweak settings
and environment variables.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:03.140
Instead, you do
a new deployment.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.300
So immutable deployments
prevent drift

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.070
in our production environment.

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:08.820
MARTIN: Ah, that
makes sense, Kevin.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:10.665
I'd love to hear
more about that.

00:04:10.665 --> 00:04:13.040
Maybe you could tell us more
details in the next episode?

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.330
KEVIN: Sounds good.

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:16.290
MARTIN: And thank you,
everyone, for watching.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:17.959
If you have questions
for Kevin or me,

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:19.829
please add them in the comments.

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:22.250
Also, this episode was a
little different from others

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:24.050
in the Serverless
Expeditions series.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.330
Please let us know if
you found it useful.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:28.190
Until next time.

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:31.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

